WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.720
This is Chapter One, Episode 2, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.320
Now, trouble in the south.

4
00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:15.682
In the first half of the 1800s, national consciousness grew in Europe, including in Denmark.

5
00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:21.358
So did the conflict between those who felt Danish and those who felt German in the Danish kingdom.

6
00:00:21.358 --> 00:00:32.745
In 1848 to 50 this triggered a civil war between the Kingdom of Denmark and German-minded rebels in the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein, where the Danish king was also ruler.

7
00:00:32.745 --> 00:00:36.770
Denmark won the war at first, but the problem was not solved.

8
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:41.558
In 1864, the national tensions again triggered a war.

9
00:00:41.558 --> 00:00:46.770
This time the Germans won, and Denmark lost both Schleswig and Holstein.

10
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.145
Let's go through it carefully.

11
00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:59.295
In the middle of the 1800s, the Danish composite state, the helstat, consisted of the Danish kingdom and the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein.

12
00:00:59.295 --> 00:01:02.820
The Danish king ruled in all three parts of the realm.

13
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:11.308
The increased national feeling and democratic ideas created tension between the Danish-speaking and the German-speaking parts of the population.

14
00:01:11.308 --> 00:01:21.708
The population in Holstein was German, and most Danish politicians saw it as natural that the Holsteiners wanted to break away from Denmark and join the German Confederation.

15
00:01:21.708 --> 00:01:33.507
But in Schleswig there were both Danish speakers, who wanted to remain part of Denmark, and German-speaking inhabitants, who wanted to break away and join the German Confederation together with Holstein.

16
00:01:33.508 --> 00:01:35.758
This was called the Schleswig problem.

17
00:01:35.758 --> 00:01:41.172
People and politicians on both sides of the border discussed where the Danish border should run.

18
00:01:41.722 --> 00:01:48.947
In 1848, Schleswig and Holstein were refused their demand for an independent free constitution.

19
00:01:48.897 --> 00:02:00.747
On the other hand, it was clear that Denmark wanted the Danish constitution, which was on its way, to also apply to Schleswig, which would then be united with Denmark but separated from Holstein.

20
00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:07.709
These conflicting wishes led to war between Schleswig and Holstein rebels on one side and Denmark on the other.

21
00:02:07.709 --> 00:02:18.030
The German Confederation and the strongest of the German states, Prussia, backed the rebels and sent a large number of soldiers into the war on the side of the duchies.

22
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:23.805
In 1849 Denmark and the Schleswig-Holsteiners fought a series of battles.

23
00:02:23.755 --> 00:02:28.767
One was at Fredericia in July, where the Danish army was victorious.

24
00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:37.130
In 1850, the German Confederation and Prussia were pressured by Russia to withdraw their auxiliary troops from the conflict.

25
00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:44.080
Afterwards the Danish army won a series of decisive victories, among others at Isted in July 1850.

26
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.517
It was the bloodiest battle of the war, with thousands of dead and wounded.

27
00:02:48.517 --> 00:02:56.980
After mediation by the European great powers, the result was that the two duchies would continue to be part of the Danish composite state.

28
00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.917
That solution would prove not to last long.

29
00:02:59.917 --> 00:03:05.424
The three-year war from 1848 to 50 is also called the First Schleswig War.

30
00:03:05.974 --> 00:03:07.524
Then came the Second.

31
00:03:07.474 --> 00:03:16.474
After the First Schleswig War, Denmark had promised the great powers that Denmark would not bind Schleswig closer to itself than Holstein.

32
00:03:16.474 --> 00:03:18.736
But Denmark broke its promise.

33
00:03:18.736 --> 00:03:29.161
In 1863, pressed by public opinion, the Danish Rigsdag adopted a new joint constitution for both Denmark and Schleswig, but not for Holstein.

34
00:03:29.161 --> 00:03:34.911
The German states saw the Danish-Schleswig constitution as a breach of the agreement that had been made.

35
00:03:34.911 --> 00:03:45.386
Prussia, whose political leader was the powerful prime minister Otto von Bismarck, therefore declared war on Denmark together with Austria in February 1864.

36
00:03:45.386 --> 00:03:48.450
This war is called the Second Schleswig War.

37
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:57.237
Unlike the First Schleswig War in 1848 to 50, this time Denmark did not get help from the European great powers.

38
00:03:57.187 --> 00:04:04.137
Denmark was attacked from the south, and the Danish forces expected that Dannevirke would protect them against the attack.

39
00:04:04.137 --> 00:04:10.500
Dannevirke was a defensive structure which, since the Viking Age, had been a strong line of defence.

40
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:19.620
But in February 1864, the Danish army had to be evacuated in great haste from Dannevirke, because the opponents outnumbered them.

41
00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:23.545
Parts of the Danish army withdrew north into Jutland.

42
00:04:23.495 --> 00:04:28.307
But the main part of the soldiers held the defensive position at Dybbøl, near Sønderborg.

43
00:04:28.307 --> 00:04:36.045
On the eighteenth of April, the Prussians attacked Dybbøl after a long bombardment, and the inferior Danish forces were defeated.

44
00:04:36.045 --> 00:04:47.550
All of Jutland was occupied by enemy forces, and when the Prussians at the end of June also conquered the island of Als, the Danish politicians had to realise that the war was lost.

45
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.275
The defeat in 1864 was a catastrophe for Denmark.

46
00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:58.600
Denmark had to cede Schleswig, Holstein and the small duchy of Lauenburg to Prussia and Austria.

47
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:19.032
The Danish king had at a stroke lost about two fifths of his population and one third of his territory, which afterwards consisted of Denmark proper, plus a number of sparsely populated lands: Greenland, the Faroe Islands and Iceland, and, until 1917, the three small Danish West Indian islands.

48
00:05:19.582 --> 00:05:25.207
So after 1864 the kingdom had a more uniform population than ever before.

49
00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:36.707
The great majority of the population spoke the same language, had the same constitution, and were culturally quite alike, though there was still a great difference between rich and poor.

50
00:05:36.707 --> 00:05:46.694
In the adversity after the defeat in 1864, the Danish national identity grew even stronger, and language and culture came to mean a lot.

51
00:05:46.694 --> 00:05:52.444
Many South Jutlanders now lived in the German empire south of the border, but felt Danish.

52
00:05:52.444 --> 00:05:58.986
Only in 1920 were the Danish-minded areas in the northern part of Schleswig reunited with Denmark.

53
00:05:59.536 --> 00:06:02.973
And what did the defeat mean for Denmark's place in the world?

54
00:06:02.923 --> 00:06:14.286
The war and the loss of the duchies in 1864 meant that Denmark was now unconditionally reduced to a small state with no influence on the decisions of the European great powers.

55
00:06:14.286 --> 00:06:24.636
In 1871, Bismarck had gathered the German states into a strong empire, and Denmark now stood completely in the shadow of its large neighbour to the south.

56
00:06:24.636 --> 00:06:29.386
There was now great concern that Denmark would be wiped out as an independent state.

57
00:06:29.386 --> 00:06:36.873
To secure its continued survival, Denmark tried at almost any price to avoid involvement in international conflicts.

58
00:06:36.873 --> 00:06:44.796
In security policy, Denmark afterwards chose to be strictly neutral, and maintained only a weak military defence.

59
00:06:45.346 --> 00:06:48.196
So here is the quick recap for Episode Two.

60
00:06:48.146 --> 00:06:54.533
Norway was in union with Denmark from 1380 to 1814, over four hundred years.

61
00:06:54.533 --> 00:06:57.858
The stavnsbånd limited the peasants' freedom to move.

62
00:06:57.858 --> 00:07:02.008
Under the 1849 constitution, women had no vote.

63
00:07:02.008 --> 00:07:10.908
Colonies: Tranquebar and Serampore in India, forts in Ghana, and the West Indian islands, sold to the USA in 1917.

64
00:07:10.908 --> 00:07:16.646
The slave trade was banned from 1803, and slavery ended in 1848.

65
00:07:16.646 --> 00:07:22.258
The stavnsbånd came in 1733, and was abolished from 1788.

66
00:07:22.258 --> 00:07:25.821
Struensee governed from 1770 to 72.

67
00:07:25.821 --> 00:07:34.608
The Battle of the Roads was in 1801, the Bombardment of Copenhagen in 1807, and Norway was lost in 1814.

68
00:07:34.608 --> 00:07:39.046
The constitution was signed on the fifth of June, 1849.

69
00:07:39.046 --> 00:07:48.633
The First Schleswig War ran from 1848 to 50, and the Second in 1864, with the defeat at Dybbøl on the eighteenth of April.

70
00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:51.870
In Episode Three, Denmark modernises.

71
00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.020
Now it is time for your exam practice.

72
00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:59.882
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

73
00:07:59.882 --> 00:08:07.368
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

74
00:08:07.918 --> 00:08:09.168
Question 1.

75
00:08:09.118 --> 00:08:12.430
Which historical event took place in 1864?

76
00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:16.205
Option A: Denmark lost the Second Schleswig War.

77
00:08:16.205 --> 00:08:20.880
Option B: Denmark got its first Social Democratic prime minister.

78
00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.522
Option C: Danish women got the vote for the Folketing.
[DA] Hvilken historisk begivenhed fandt sted i 1864?  A: Danmark tabte Den 2. Slesvigske Krig  |  B: Danmark fik sin første socialdemokratiske statsminister  |  C: Danske kvinder fik valgret til Folketinget

79
00:08:31.072 --> 00:08:35.072
The answer is A: Denmark lost the Second Schleswig War.

80
00:08:35.022 --> 00:08:39.420
It appeared in the June 2021 and November 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Danmark tabte Den 2. Slesvigske Krig

81
00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.220
Question 2.

82
00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:45.157
What was the result of the Second Schleswig War in 1864?

83
00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:48.357
Option A: Denmark conquered Schleswig.

84
00:08:48.357 --> 00:08:51.294
Option B: Denmark lost Schleswig.
[DA] Hvad blev resultatet af Den 2. Slesvigske Krig i 1864?  A: Danmark erobrede Slesvig  |  B: Danmark tabte Slesvig

85
00:08:57.844 --> 00:09:01.031
The answer is B: Denmark lost Schleswig.

86
00:09:00.981 --> 00:09:03.648
It appeared in the May 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Danmark tabte Slesvig

87
00:09:04.198 --> 00:09:05.960
That's the end of this part.

88
00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:06.978
Well done.

